 "_comment": "Five more seeds on Computers and CS, both arms, at the 400-epoch protocol.\n\nPURPOSE IS STATISTICAL POWER, NOT A BETTER MEAN. Both datasets are unanimous\nat five seeds - Computers 5/5 (+0.0634 ROC, +0.1107 PR), CS 5/5 (+0.0167,\n+0.0349) - and both report p=0.0625, which is not a marginal result but the\nFLOOR of the two-sided Wilcoxon at n=5: 2^-4. No effect size, however large,\ncan reach p<0.05 with five pairs. At n=10 a unanimous result gives p=0.002.\nThe two cleanest wins in the paper currently cannot be starred for a reason\nthat has nothing to do with the evidence, and reviewers count stars.\n\nSeeds 4-8 extend the existing 0,1,2,3,42. E400 tags, because the uniform\n400-epoch protocol is the one the paper should report: at 200 epochs our DEMO\narm is undertrained (77% of the Photo reproduction gap closes by going to 400,\nanalysis/tables/demo_gap_by_budget.csv), so a 200-epoch head-to-head compares\nagainst a baseline a reviewer can dismiss.\n\nThe `set` blocks are COPIED FROM THE EXISTING SHARDS' recorded config, not\nretyped: the E400 DEMO arm runs energy_loss=False while config.json's\ndemo_default says True, so a hand-written spec would have extended the group\nwith a different baseline and reported the mixture as one number. Same class of\nfailure as the mixup default that made every pre-2026-08-20 DEMO arm an\nablation.\n\nMeasured from the existing E400 shards, not estimated: computers 23.5/19.5 min\nper rotation (outpost/demo), cs 77.2/77.9 min. 130 rotation-jobs, ~121\nGPU-hours. cs is budgeted a whole card - demo peaks at 28.8 GB allocated.",
